1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,636
When a mother sees her child moving toward danger, surely she pulls him back.

2
00:00:07,636 --> 00:00:21,239
Yet in Leonardo da Vinci’s The Virgin and Child with Saint Anne, Mary reaches forward and leans toward Jesus without fully stopping him.

3
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:23,983
The child plays with a lamb.

4
00:00:23,983 --> 00:00:33,886
To a Christian viewer, however, the lamb also evokes the Lamb of God and Christ’s future sacrifice.

5
00:00:33,886 --> 00:00:48,920
A gentle family scene therefore becomes an unsettling question: is Mary trying to protect her son, or learning to accept a destiny she cannot prevent?

6
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,500
Leonardo offers no written answer.

7
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:03,000
He lets arms, glances, smiles, and interlocking bodies hold love, resistance, and surrender together.

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,150
The Louvre dates Leonardo’s painting to about 1503–1519.

9
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:20,026
It is oil on a poplar panel, now approximately 168 centimeters high and 130 centimeters wide, and carries the inventory number INV 776.

10
00:01:20,026 --> 00:01:29,485
It hangs in the Grande Galerie on the first floor of the Denon wing, beside the room containing the Mona Lisa.

11
00:01:29,485 --> 00:01:44,094
If the Mona Lisa concentrates mystery within one face, Saint Anne distributes uncertainty across four bodies: Anne, her daughter Mary, the Christ Child, and a lamb.

12
00:01:44,094 --> 00:01:50,085
This was not a picture completed in a single campaign and delivered.

13
00:01:50,085 --> 00:01:56,181
Leonardo adjusted figures, drapery, and landscape over many years.

14
00:01:56,181 --> 00:02:03,853
When he died in 1519, parts of the foreground and terrain remained visibly unfinished.

15
00:02:03,853 --> 00:02:11,000
The painting is both a masterpiece and the record of an extended working process.

16
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,742
Saint Anne is Mary’s mother and Christ’s grandmother.

17
00:02:15,742 --> 00:02:25,225
Some traditions held that Anne died before Christ’s birth, so this gathering is not ordinary family history.

18
00:02:25,225 --> 00:02:34,192
It is a theological construction that brings lineage, motherhood, and redemption into the same space.

19
00:02:34,192 --> 00:02:37,182
Anne sits higher and farther back.

20
00:02:37,182 --> 00:02:43,572
Mary appears almost to sit across her mother’s lap while leaning forward.

21
00:02:43,572 --> 00:02:46,768
Christ twists toward the lamb below.

22
00:02:46,768 --> 00:02:56,766
The generations are not displayed side by side; movement seems to pass from Anne to Mary, and then from Mary to Jesus.

23
00:02:56,766 --> 00:03:04,806
The oldest figure is the most stable. The mother is suspended between holding and releasing.

24
00:03:04,806 --> 00:03:09,548
The child moves most energetically toward the future.

25
00:03:09,548 --> 00:03:18,000
Leonardo compresses years of family and sacred history into the bodily rhythm of a single second.

26
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:29,078
At first, the figures form a stable pyramid, with Anne’s head at the summit and Mary and Christ expanding below.

27
00:03:29,078 --> 00:03:36,583
Look longer, however, and Mary’s position becomes surprisingly strange.

28
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:47,781
She seems to sit across Anne’s legs, rotate her pelvis, lean forward, and extend both arms in another direction.

29
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:55,047
Leonardo did not organize the body according to an ordinary seated pose.

30
00:03:55,047 --> 00:03:58,502
Every joint serves the narrative.

31
00:03:58,502 --> 00:04:12,915
Anne supplies the stable anchor; Mary becomes the active hinge between generations; Christ carries the accumulated movement toward the lamb.

32
00:04:12,915 --> 00:04:23,279
Sfumato softens the boundaries so that the bodies merge through subtle transitions of light and shade.

33
00:04:23,279 --> 00:04:34,000
A pose that is highly artificial, even nearly impossible, begins to feel graceful, continuous, and alive.

34
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,875
Jesus does not sit quietly in Mary’s arms.

35
00:04:38,875 --> 00:04:50,575
He presses against or straddles the lamb, holds its neck, and turns with the energy of a child at play.

36
00:04:50,575 --> 00:04:59,768
Yet the lamb had long represented the Lamb of God—Christ offered in sacrifice.

37
00:04:59,768 --> 00:05:07,150
Leonardo therefore places the future inside a childhood game.

38
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:15,089
Jesus seems both to embrace the lamb and to accept what it signifies.

39
00:05:15,089 --> 00:05:29,157
Mary’s approaching hands can be read as an effort to pull him away, or as hesitation between maternal love and obedience.

40
00:05:29,157 --> 00:05:39,186
There is no cross, blade, or blood in the painting, but the Passion is already present.

41
00:05:39,186 --> 00:05:52,000
It has been condensed into the small distance between a mother’s fingers and a child she cannot quite reclaim.

42
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,764
Anne watches her daughter and grandson with a calm, elusive smile.

43
00:05:58,764 --> 00:06:08,064
Mary is absorbed in the child’s movement, expressing tenderness but perhaps also anxiety.

44
00:06:08,064 --> 00:06:15,915
Jesus turns back toward his mother even as his body continues toward the lamb.

45
00:06:15,915 --> 00:06:20,867
The three faces seem to inhabit different times.

46
00:06:20,867 --> 00:06:34,757
Anne may suggest a longer sacred order; Mary occupies the immediate tension of maternal love; Jesus moves physically toward the future.

47
00:06:34,757 --> 00:06:43,694
Still, no document proves that Leonardo intended Anne to be the one who “already knows.”

48
00:06:43,694 --> 00:07:00,000
What he gives us is a precise choreography of glances and expressions that remain open enough for affection, sorrow, understanding, and submission to coexist.

49
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:08,996
Many viewers notice that the two adult women seem to possess only three clearly readable legs.

50
00:07:08,996 --> 00:07:17,310
Some then connect the number three to the Trinity and treat it as a hidden Leonardo code.

51
00:07:17,310 --> 00:07:30,520
A more direct explanation lies in the overlap of Mary’s blue mantle, Anne’s drapery, underlying lines, and incompletely resolved paint.

52
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:42,363
Leonardo left no statement identifying a deliberate three-legged design, and technical evidence does not establish one.

53
00:07:42,363 --> 00:07:54,206
The integration of three generations into an almost indivisible group may certainly evoke a visual sense of “three in one.”

54
00:07:54,206 --> 00:08:04,000
But that is a later theological association, not a secret that scholarship has conclusively decoded.

55
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,010
Leonardo’s sfumato is not simply blur placed around the figures.

56
00:08:11,010 --> 00:08:16,108
Fine tonal transitions weaken fixed contours.

57
00:08:16,108 --> 00:08:31,784
Cheeks dissolve into shadow, mouths lack a hard line that could lock an emotion in place, and arms and drapery seem to emerge gradually through air.

58
00:08:31,784 --> 00:08:39,941
That is why Anne and Mary cannot be classified simply as smiling or grieving.

59
00:08:39,941 --> 00:08:48,098
Emotion appears as an ongoing formation rather than a completed statement.

60
00:08:48,098 --> 00:08:59,696
The landscape uses atmospheric perspective: distant mountains become bluer, lighter, and less distinct.

61
00:08:59,696 --> 00:09:09,127
Rocks and water also reflect Leonardo’s long interest in geology and natural movement.

62
00:09:09,127 --> 00:09:15,245
The figures do not sit like cut-outs before a background.

63
00:09:15,245 --> 00:09:22,000
Body, air, and terrain seem to participate in one slow current.

64
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:29,347
The faces, hands, and parts of the clothing are astonishingly refined.

65
00:09:29,347 --> 00:09:42,449
Yet the foreground, rocky stream, and middle ground between the figures and blue mountains remain substantially unfinished.

66
00:09:42,449 --> 00:09:52,245
This is not simply the result of later damage; the painting itself stopped at different stages.

67
00:09:52,245 --> 00:10:13,306
Infrared reflectography, X-rays, ultraviolet fluorescence, and material analysis show that Leonardo continued to revise drapery, hairstyles, gestures, and relationships after painting had begun.

68
00:10:13,306 --> 00:10:19,918
He did not finish a perfect drawing and merely fill it with color.

69
00:10:19,918 --> 00:10:29,469
Thought continued inside the paint layers. The work is therefore both image and laboratory.

70
00:10:29,469 --> 00:10:40,000
Areas that were never fully covered allow us to watch an artist repeatedly refuse his previous answer.

71
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:59,136
Vasari wrote that friars at Santissima Annunziata in Florence waited for Leonardo to develop a Saint Anne composition, and that a publicly displayed cartoon drew crowds for two days.

72
00:10:59,136 --> 00:11:14,544
The story shows that the design attracted enormous attention, but no surviving contract proves that the Louvre panel was the intended altarpiece.

73
00:11:14,544 --> 00:11:33,556
Another older theory proposed that Louis XII commissioned the painting to celebrate the birth of his daughter Claude, partly because Queen Anne of Brittany shared the saint’s name.

74
00:11:33,556 --> 00:11:41,260
Current Louvre scholarship does not treat this as established evidence.

75
00:11:41,260 --> 00:11:58,657
The most honest conclusion is that Saint Anne had strong religious visibility in Florence around 1500, and the painting later became connected with the French court.

76
00:11:58,657 --> 00:12:04,000
The original patron, however, remains uncertain.

77
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:17,221
The National Gallery’s Burlington House Cartoon shows Anne, Mary, Christ, and the young John the Baptist—without a lamb.

78
00:12:17,221 --> 00:12:33,009
The museum currently dates it to about 1506–1508 and describes it as a full-scale presentation drawing rather than an ordinary transfer cartoon.

79
00:12:33,009 --> 00:12:43,791
On 3 April 1501, Fra’ Pietro da Novellara described another Saint Anne cartoon that included a lamb.

80
00:12:43,791 --> 00:12:45,845
That sheet is lost.

81
00:12:45,845 --> 00:13:06,896
From the letter and later copies, scholars reconstruct a design in which Mary’s effort to restrain Christ, and Anne’s effort to restrain Mary, may have been more explicit than in the Louvre panel.

82
00:13:06,896 --> 00:13:14,725
This is not a simple straight line, and not merely John replaced by a lamb.

83
00:13:14,725 --> 00:13:22,042
Leonardo repeatedly tested figures, orientations, and gestures.

84
00:13:22,042 --> 00:13:30,000
The surviving painting preserves one late state of a much longer process.

85
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:46,969
In 2005, scholars identified a note written in October 1503 by the Florentine official Agostino Vespucci in a volume of Cicero at Heidelberg University Library.

86
00:13:46,969 --> 00:14:02,815
Vespucci compared Leonardo to the ancient painter Apelles and mentioned both the head of Lisa del Giocondo and the head of “Anne, mother of the Virgin.”

87
00:14:02,815 --> 00:14:12,672
This brief note gives the Mona Lisa and the Saint Anne project a rare contemporary coordinate.

88
00:14:12,672 --> 00:14:22,154
It does not describe the entire composition or tell us which state of the painting existed.

89
00:14:22,154 --> 00:14:38,000
What it demonstrates is that by 1503, these two subjects—later among Leonardo’s most famous works—were already present together in his working life.

90
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:48,595
Leonardo probably began the panel in Florence and continued to revise it while moving between cities.

91
00:14:48,595 --> 00:14:52,291
In 1516 or 1517 he settled in France.

92
00:14:52,291 --> 00:15:07,075
In October 1517, Antonio de Beatis recorded seeing a painting of the Virgin, Christ Child, and Saint Anne at Leonardo’s residence at Clos Lucé.

93
00:15:07,075 --> 00:15:19,149
The Louvre considers it likely that Francis I acquired the work around 1518, but no complete sale contract survives.

94
00:15:19,149 --> 00:15:30,853
Large gaps remain after Leonardo’s death; a royal inventory of 1683 offers another clearer point in the history.

95
00:15:30,853 --> 00:15:38,984
After the French Revolution, the royal collection became national property.

96
00:15:38,984 --> 00:15:55,000
When the Louvre opened to the public in 1793, the painting’s identity gradually shifted from a monarch’s possession to a work of public cultural heritage.

97
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:06,194
Examination in 2008 and the conservation campaign begun in 2009 directed new attention to the back of the panel.

98
00:16:06,194 --> 00:16:17,032
Beyond a horse’s head already visible to the eye, infrared imaging revealed a skull and the figure of a child.

99
00:16:17,032 --> 00:16:30,370
The lines demonstrate that the reverse was used for drawing, but they do not prove that all three images were made by Leonardo himself.

100
00:16:30,370 --> 00:16:37,753
The child should not be identified automatically as Christ with the lamb.

101
00:16:37,753 --> 00:16:42,159
The uncertainty is part of the fascination.

102
00:16:42,159 --> 00:17:03,000
One of the most intensively studied artists in history could still leave unidentified lines on the back of a panel that had been viewed for centuries, waiting for scientific imaging to make them visible again.

103
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:12,618
From 2009 to 2012, the Louvre and C2RMF conducted a major conservation and research campaign.

104
00:17:12,618 --> 00:17:23,211
Cleaning made the complex ultramarine layers and color relationships in Mary’s garment more visible.

105
00:17:23,211 --> 00:17:32,707
Research also clarified unfinished areas, later overpaint, and the condition of the paint.

106
00:17:32,707 --> 00:17:39,160
Conservators did not simply remove every later intervention.

107
00:17:39,160 --> 00:18:00,953
Critics including Ségolène Bergeon Langle and Jean-Pierre Cuzin worried that cleaning went too far, perhaps removing a white layer from Christ’s body or weakening the smoky effect created by Leonardo’s glazes.

108
00:18:00,953 --> 00:18:15,685
It is therefore inaccurate to claim either that the painting was restored perfectly to its original state or that it was unquestionably ruined.

109
00:18:15,685 --> 00:18:32,000
Conservation revealed some relationships while sharpening an unavoidable question: after five centuries, what exactly counts as the original appearance?

110
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:51,302
In 1910, Sigmund Freud connected the similarly aged Anne and Mary—the two mothers visually intertwined—with Leonardo’s separation from his birth mother and upbringing within his father’s household.

111
00:18:51,302 --> 00:18:57,059
This became the influential “two mothers” interpretation.

112
00:18:57,059 --> 00:19:05,412
Freud also saw a vulture in Mary’s drapery and linked it to Egyptian maternal mythology.

113
00:19:05,412 --> 00:19:14,104
But the Italian word in Leonardo’s remembered passage, nibbio, means a kite, not a vulture.

114
00:19:14,104 --> 00:19:20,538
The translation mistake seriously weakened the vulture argument.

115
00:19:20,538 --> 00:19:31,149
This does not make every connection between Leonardo’s life and the image’s multiple motherhood meaningless.

116
00:19:31,149 --> 00:19:38,712
It reminds us that even a compelling interpretation may rest on a mistaken word.

117
00:19:38,712 --> 00:19:50,000
Biography can open a route into the painting, but it cannot replace historical, iconographic, and material evidence.

118
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:57,676
The Virgin and Child with Saint Anne appears to show three generations of a family.

119
00:19:57,676 --> 00:20:08,578
Beneath that surface, it compresses family, nature, salvation, and artistic creation into one suspended instant.

120
00:20:08,578 --> 00:20:17,478
Anne does not clearly restrain her daughter. Mary reaches forward but does not take Jesus away.

121
00:20:17,478 --> 00:20:24,932
The child looks back toward his mother while his body continues toward the lamb.

122
00:20:24,932 --> 00:20:32,497
Everyone is joined by love, yet everyone is being carried toward the next moment.

123
00:20:32,497 --> 00:20:41,731
Leonardo’s achievement is not to explain theology neatly, but to turn love itself into a question.

124
00:20:41,731 --> 00:20:47,849
Does protection have a limit? Can letting go also be an act of love?

125
00:20:47,849 --> 00:20:58,195
When we cannot walk another person’s path for them, does the hand we still extend mean resistance—or blessing?

126
00:20:58,195 --> 00:21:05,871
Perhaps that is why the painting has refused to become quiet after five centuries.

127
00:21:05,871 --> 00:21:10,878
It does not remain safely inside a story about saints.

128
00:21:10,878 --> 00:21:20,000
It draws into the image everyone who has ever tried to protect, release, or lose someone they love.

129
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,347
This is Our Famous Artists.

130
00:21:22,347 --> 00:21:30,000
If you enjoy looking again with evidence, subscribe and join us for the next masterpiece.
